```

This limits the number of code points to < 255. But when converted to UTF-8, a single code point can be multiple bytes! So 254 code points could easily exceed 256 bytes in UTF-8.

Specifically, a code point in the range U+0080-U+07FF takes 2 UTF-8 bytes. A code point in U+0800-U+FFFF takes 3 bytes. And U+10000-U+10FFFF takes 4 bytes.

So with 254 code points at 3 bytes each, the UTF-8 output could be 762 bytes, far exceeding the 256-byte `_mapped` buffer!

